alex: Hey — hitting a weird interop issue when booting the JS SDK inside an Edge function (Next.js). I get `SyntaxError: Cannot use import statement outside a module` even though my project is ESM.

maria: Are you shipping the ESM build from node_modules? The edge runtime requires ESM only, but some bundlers end up pulling the CJS entry. What's your import line and package.json `type`?

alex: package.json has "type": "module". I do `import Redwood from '@redwood/inference'` (example name). Minimal repro: ```js
// edge/route.js
import Redwood from '@redwood/inference'
export default async (req) => {
  const r = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_KEY })
  return new Response(await r.generate('hi'))
}
```
Error points at the SDK's `require()` usage in a transitive file.

sam: Ah that sounds like the package.json exports map maybe points to a cjs entry for node resolution. Edge bundlers (Vercel/Workers) prefer the `exports` -> `import` entry. Do you have a lockfile that pins an older build?

jin: Also watch out: process.env isn't populated in Vercel Edge runtime the same way. You might need to pass secrets via Vercel's runtime config or use an inline header.

alex: Good catch. I tested `globalThis.process = { env: { REDWOOD_KEY: 'x' } }` and auth moves forward but that feels gross. If the SDK uses `require` behind the scenes it breaks ESM bundles.

maria: For typings/TS: if consumers try `const Redwood = require('@redwood/inference')` TS will complain about default vs named exports. Recommend adding examples for both: `import Redwood from` and `import * as Redwood from` depending on bundler.

sam: Quick workaround for now: force the ESM entry by importing the module path directly (if published): `import Redwood from '@redwood/inference/dist/esm/index.mjs'` — not ideal but gets you unblocked.

jin: I filed a small issue: https://github.com/redwood-inference/js-sdk/issues/142 — notes: ensure package.json has `exports.{"import"}` and `module` field, ship a pure ESM build for edge, and add a small runtime-agnostic auth helper (accepts header or env) so edge customers know to use `fetch` header mode.

alex: Thanks. One more oddity — when I bundle for Cloudflare with esbuild, TS complains: "Could not find a declaration file for module '@redwood/inference'". So maybe types not referenced in the ESM entry.

maria: Right — so action items: 1) docs: explicit Edge section covering import styles, process.env caveats, and header-based auth sample; 2) package: verify `exports` map + publish types for ESM entry; 3) add a small note about retries failing in worker runtimes because `setTimeout`/`fetch` semantics differ.

alex: I'll open a docs PR with a snippet for Edge and the auth header example. Quick snippet to include: ```js
// edge.js (recommended)
export default async (req) => {
  const apiKey = req.headers.get('x-redwood-key') // use platform secret injection
  const r = new Redwood({ apiKey, fetchFn: fetch }) // pass fetch for runtime consistency
  return new Response(await r.generate('hello'))
}
```

sam: Great. devx-bot: linking current docs + the issue: <https://redwood.inference/docs/js-sdk-edge> and https://github.com/redwood-inference/js-sdk/issues/142 :thumbsup:

devx-bot: Added label `edge-docs` and assigned to @alex.

alex: Perfect — thanks everyone. Will post PR link when ready.
